@flexiarg argument-coherence/earn-the-coined-term
@title Earn the Coined Term
@keywords coinage, new term, salience, justification, sales pitch
@audience paper and proposal authors, reviewers, who-benefits analysts
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Mined from the Peeragogy "Greatest Flops" archive (~/notes/peeragogy-flops): the three LAK 2011 reviews of flop 1 (appendices/01, 'Reviews'); Joe's five-question analysis of flop 1 in the outtakes (appendices/07). Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; to test blind on the other flops, with Corneli 2016 (ICCC) and the Peeragogy Handbook as controls that should pass
@references [argument-coherence/ARGUMENT argument-coherence/evidence-the-venue-counts argument-coherence/one-worked-instance argument-coherence/say-how-it-carries-back argument-coherence/show-the-deliverable argument-coherence/plan-at-reviewable-grain argument-coherence/claims-as-hypotheses]

! conclusion:
  A new term for the central idea must show what it lets the reader see or do that existing terms do not, early and with an example; otherwise reviewers read it as a relabelling.

  + context:
    Coining a term feels like progress to its authors, who know what it gathers. A reader meets only the word.

  + IF:
    The argument introduces a new name for its main idea.

  + HOWEVER:
    Some ideas do need a new name, and a defensive comparison with every neighbour wastes the reader's time.

  + THEN:
    State the difference from the nearest existing term in one or two sentences, with one case where the difference changes what someone does.

  + BECAUSE:
    Flop 1 introduced 'paragogy' by adapting Knowles's andragogy. Joe's reading of the reviews: 'Is the concept of paragogy needed? Is it salient? Or is this "a proposal for a learning-analytical pedagogy" hiding behind an overblown sales pitch?' Review 1: 'The justification for "Paragogy" does not appear to be strong.'

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Test blind: a reviewer given only a flop and this family; compare its rows with the recorded verdicts.]
    next[Related: writing-coherence/plain-language-thesis and first-mention-undischarged cover legibility; this covers whether the term is needed.]
